---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "../../components/docs/Code.astro";
import DocShot from "../../components/docs/DocShot.astro";
import Cards from "../../components/docs/Cards.astro";

// Every output below is real: a coding.sh issue-triage prompt (coding-sh-triage, 10 issue
// titles from the repo) run with the packed CLI against scripts/e2e/stub-model.mjs under a
// throwaway HOME and CODING_SH_HOME, 2026-11-11. Ids are kept as printed; long tables are cut at "…".

const cases = `{"input":"Viewer search misses tool output","expected":"bug, viewer"}
{"input":"Hook drops events when the spool is full","expected":"bug, capture"}
{"input":"Cost shows $0 for cached tokens","expected":"bug, cost"}`;

const importCmd = `coding.sh dataset import cases.jsonl --name triage`;

const spec = `{
  "dataset": "triage",
  "task": { "kind": "command", "command": "node triage.mjs", "env": { "PROMPT": "prompt-v1.md" } },
  "graders": [{ "kind": "exact" }]
}`;

const runCmd = `coding.sh exp run before.json --name before
coding.sh exp run after.json --name after`;

const runOut = `after  exp_01M752PE53DAYRTDFP98M9P9EF
dataset triage@f833e97842bc5b89  task command: node triage.mjs  trials 1
pass    70.0% [39.7%, 89.2%] (7/10, 95% Wilson)
latency median 1.1s, p90 1.2s, max 1.4s

failures 3
case                reason
------------------  ------------------------------------------------
c_e50657b42d139286  exact: expected "bug, cost", got "feature, cost"
c_bd29d0801ad073e6  exact: expected "bug, otel", got "feature, otel"
c_0a2156109efb3003  exact: expected "feature, cost", got "feature"`;

const compareCmd = `coding.sh exp compare before after`;

const compareOut = `A before exp_01M752P8T3TPFNSVRKYJ1D8C1N  pass 0.0%
B after exp_01M752PE53DAYRTDFP98M9P9EF  pass 70.0%
! A and B ran one after the other, not interleaved, so drift during the runs lands on one arm; run both in one interleaved job: coding.sh exp run --arm a=<TASK> --arm b=<TASK>

B − A   +70.0pp [+40.0pp, +100.0pp] (10 paired cases, 95% paired bootstrap)
mcnemar p=0.016  0 regressed, 7 improved  sign-flip p=0.016 over 10 cases
…
verdict B is better
…
! only 10 paired cases (< 200): small suites detect only large effects and their intervals can under-cover; add cases or trials before trusting a null result

improvements 7
case                 A     B       Δ
------------------  --  ----  ------
c_1b9bbea8b6497170  0%  100%  +100pp
c_7699d4aa57c4f862  0%  100%  +100pp
…`;

const regradeCmd = `coding.sh exp regrade after -g area.json --replace-graders`;

const regradeOut = `regrade exp_01M752RR4DNQZ60NTFYNF2DV6D of exp_01M752PE53DAYRTDFP98M9P9EF; the task was not called
pass    70.0% → 90.0% (+20.0pp) (7/10 → 9/10 graded runs)
grader  exact 48fe67515f98 → — (dropped)
grader  area — → d7a4f11c4da2 (new)
flips   2 of 10 runs: 0 pass → fail, 2 fail → pass`;

const armsCmd = `coding.sh exp run -d triage -g '{"kind":"exact"}' --arm before=before.json --arm after=after.json`;

const sweepCmd = `coding.sh exp sweep v1=before.json v2=after.json hot=hot.json -d triage -g '{"kind":"exact"}'`;

const matrix = `{
  "name": "triage-matrix",
  "dataset": "triage",
  "task": { "kind": "command", "command": "node triage.mjs", "env": { "PROMPT": "prompt-v1.md", "TEMPERATURE": "0" } },
  "graders": [{ "kind": "exact" }],
  "matrix": {
    "prompt": { "path": "/task/env/PROMPT", "values": [{ "v1": "prompt-v1.md" }, { "v2": "prompt-v2.md" }] },
    "temp": { "path": "/task/env/TEMPERATURE", "values": [{ "t0": "0" }, { "t1": "1" }] }
  },
  "baseline": { "prompt": "v1", "temp": "t0" }
}`;

const matrixOut = `matrix mx_01M752SGFEXP3FMPWG8NR50PCN  4 cells, interleaved (seed 1281647668); baseline prompt=v1,temp=t0

cell                pass     B − A  95% CI                   p  p (Holm)  regressed  improved  verdict
-----------------  -----  --------  -------------------  -----  --------  ---------  --------  ------------
prompt=v1,temp=t0   0.0%  baseline
prompt=v1,temp=t1   0.0%     0.0pp  [0.0pp, 0.0pp]       1.000     1.000          0         0  inconclusive
prompt=v2,temp=t0  70.0%   +70.0pp  [+40.0pp, +100.0pp]  0.016     0.047          0         7  better
prompt=v2,temp=t1  60.0%   +60.0pp  [+30.0pp, +90.0pp]   0.031     0.063          0         6  inconclusive
plausibly best (95%, 10 shared cases): prompt=v2,temp=t0, prompt=v2,temp=t1  (configs not significantly worse than the best, paired-bootstrap MCB)`;

const plateauCmd = `coding.sh exp run spec.json --name v5 --series triage-loop
coding.sh exp plateau --series triage-loop`;

const metricsCmd = `coding.sh exp metrics --arm base='node bench.mjs --dir a' --arm cand='node bench.mjs --dir b'`;

const replayCmd = `coding.sh exp replay latest#-1 --vary model=claude-haiku-4-5 --dry-run
coding.sh exp replay latest#-1 --vary model=claude-sonnet-4-5,claude-haiku-4-5 --n 5`;
---

<DocsLayout>
  <p>
    An experiment runs one task over every case of a dataset and grades each output. The task can be any command or HTTP endpoint: a
    prompt, an agent, a tool. Two experiments on the same dataset compare case by case.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compare">Compare two versions</h2>
  <p>
    The example labels coding.sh's own GitHub issues. <code>before</code> uses a prompt that only asks for labels; <code>after</code> also
    lists the repo's labels. Each case is an issue title and the labels it should get.
  </p>
  <Code title="cases.jsonl" code={cases} />
  <Code code={importCmd} />
  <p>
    A spec names the dataset, the task and the graders. A command task gets each case input as JSON on stdin and prints its answer; the
    <code>exact</code> grader checks it against <code>expected</code>. <code>after.json</code> is the same file with
    <code>prompt-v2.md</code>.
  </p>
  <Code title="before.json" code={spec} />
  <Code code={runCmd} />
  <Code title="output" code={runOut} />
  <p>Then compare them. A is the first experiment, B the second; names resolve to the newest experiment with that name.</p>
  <Code code={compareCmd} />
  <Code title="output" code={compareOut} />
  <p>
    B fixed 7 cases and broke none. The interval for the change in pass rate is +40 to +100 points, so the verdict is <code>B is better</code>.
    The warnings ask for more cases and for one interleaved run (<a href="#arms">below</a>).
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Verdict</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>B is better</code></td><td>The 95% interval for B − A is above zero.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>B is worse</code></td><td>The interval is below zero.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>inconclusive (CI includes 0)</code></td><td>Not enough evidence either way. Add cases or trials.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The viewer shows the same comparison, with each case's two outputs side by side.</p>
  <DocShot
    name="docs/compare"
    bar="coding.sh ui"
    alt="The viewer's compare page: experiment agents-md-today against agents-md-rewrite on the dataset coding-sh-tasks, with the pass rates, the paired change and the cases that flipped."
    caption="coding.sh's AGENTS.md today against a rewrite, on 20 coding.sh tasks."
  />
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr id="gate"><td><code>--gate</code></td><td>off</td><td>Prints Ship it, Hold or Don't ship, and exits 0 only for Ship it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--lambda</code></td><td>2</td><td>With <code>--gate</code>: what breaking one solved case costs, in fixed cases.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--gate-alpha</code></td><td>0.05</td><td>With <code>--gate</code>: the one-sided significance level.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--tau</code></td><td>0.10</td><td>With <code>--gate</code>: the largest share of A's solved cases B may break.</td></tr>
        <tr id="non-inferiority"><td><code>--margin</code></td><td>none</td><td>Non-inferiority: exits 1 unless the interval's lower end is above −D (<code>0.03</code> is 3 points).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--cluster</code></td><td>none</td><td>Resamples groups of related cases (a metadata field such as <code>repo</code>) instead of single cases.</td></tr>
        <tr id="judges"><td><code>--pairwise</code></td><td>none</td><td>A judge that sees A's and B's outputs side by side, in both orders. Repeat it to ask several.</td></tr>
        <tr id="ppi"><td><code>--labels-a</code>, <code>--labels-b</code></td><td>none</td><td>Human labels that correct each LLM judge's B − A. Needs <code>--min-labels</code> (20) per arm.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="regrade">Grade again without running the task</h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp regrade</code> grades a stored experiment's outputs with other graders and calls nothing. Here a grader that checks only the
    area label replaces <code>exact</code>:
  </p>
  <Code code={regradeCmd} />
  <Code title="output" code={regradeOut} />
  <p>
    Regrade A with B's graders before you compare them, so both sides are graded the same way. <code>exp run spec.json --watch</code>
    reruns on every change to the spec, the task script or a grader file; a change to the graders alone regrades the last outputs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="arms">Run A and B as one A/B</h2>
  <p>
    With two <code>--arm</code> flags, each case runs in both arms back to back, in random order, so drift such as a slower API hits both
    arms the same. It prints the compare report above.
  </p>
  <Code code={armsCmd} />
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--arm NAME=FILE</code></td><td>none</td><td>One arm's task spec or file. Give two: A, then B.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--stop-early</code></td><td>off</td><td>Stops once a group sequential test is decisive.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--looks</code></td><td>4</td><td>With <code>--stop-early</code>: how many times it checks over the case budget.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--max-cases</code></td><td>all</td><td>Runs at most N cases, picked at random.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--trials</code></td><td>1</td><td>Attempts per case.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sweep">Pick the best of several configs</h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp sweep</code> races configs on the <code>train</code> split, keeps the better half each round, then runs the winner and the
    first config on the <code>test</code> split, which the race never saw. It prints <code>adopt &lt;winner&gt;</code> or
    <code>keep &lt;start&gt;</code> with the wins and losses on the held-out cases.
  </p>
  <Code code={sweepCmd} />
  <p>
    <code>dataset import</code> splits cases 60/20/20 by a hash of their id, so a small dataset can end up with no test cases and the sweep
    stops with <code>has no cases in the "test" split</code>. Set <code>"split"</code> on some cases, or add more.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--budget</code></td><td>each race case once</td><td>Case runs for the race, over all configs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--split</code></td><td>train</td><td>The split the race runs on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--held-out</code></td><td>test</td><td>The split the winner is scored on again.</td></tr>
        <tr id="guard"><td><code>--guard</code></td><td>none</td><td>An earlier suite the winner must not forget (<code>prior</code>: every suite a sweep adopted on). A Don't ship there keeps the start and exits 1.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--guard-tau</code></td><td>0.10</td><td>With <code>--guard</code>: the share of the start's solved guard cases the winner may break.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="matrix">Run every combination</h2>
  <p>
    A spec with <code>"matrix"</code> runs every combination of its axes against one baseline cell. Each axis is a JSON Pointer into the spec
    and its values. A cell may change only <code>/task/…</code> and <code>/trials</code>.
  </p>
  <Code title="matrix.json" code={matrix} />
  <Code code="coding.sh exp run matrix.json" />
  <Code title="output" code={matrixOut} />
  <p>
    The p-values are adjusted for the three comparisons, so <code>prompt=v2,temp=t1</code> stays inconclusive at p 0.063. The last line
    names every cell not significantly worse than the best one.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--plan</code></td><td>off</td><td>Prints the cells and what each changes, and runs nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--cell</code></td><td>all</td><td>Runs only these cells. The baseline always runs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--reuse</code></td><td>none</td><td><code>baseline</code> or <code>all</code>: copies finished case-trials from earlier experiments instead of running them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--reuse-max-age</code></td><td>7d</td><td>With <code>--reuse</code>: only case-trials that started this recently.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="plateau">Stop a loop at a plateau</h2>
  <p>
    Tag each round of a prompt loop with <code>--series</code>. Each run then prints <code>continue</code> or <code>stop</code> and the
    version to ship. <code>exp plateau</code> prints one row per round and exits 0 to continue, 3 to stop, or 2
    with too few rounds.
  </p>
  <Code code={plateauCmd} />
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--eps</code></td><td>0.05</td><td>The smallest gain per round worth another round (5 points).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--delta</code></td><td>0.05</td><td>False alarms come on average no sooner than every 1/δ rounds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--gate</code></td><td>greedy</td><td><code>greedy</code> keeps a round only when it beats the incumbent; <code>chain</code> keeps every round.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="resume">Resume a long run</h2>
  <p>
    Each trial is saved as it finishes. After Ctrl-C or a crash, run the same command with <code>--resume</code>: only the unfinished trials
    run. If the dataset, graders or task changed, it refuses and names what changed. <code>exp status</code> shows progress, ETA and cost so
    far.
  </p>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--resume [ID]</code></td><td>off</td><td>Continues an interrupted run. With no id, the newest unfinished one with the same config.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--retry-errors</code></td><td>off</td><td>With <code>--resume</code>: also runs its task errors again.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--retries</code></td><td>3</td><td>Retries after a rate limit, 5xx or network error. Failed answers are never retried.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--stop-after-infra</code></td><td>5</td><td>Stops after this many infrastructure failures in a row. The run then gives no verdict.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--trial-timeout</code></td><td>none</td><td>Seconds per trial, retries included.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="subset">Run part of a dataset</h2>
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr id="failing"><td><code>--failing EXP</code></td><td>off</td><td>Only the cases that experiment failed. Compare then warns about regression to the mean.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--errors-from EXP</code></td><td>off</td><td>Only the cases with a task or infrastructure error there.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--case</code></td><td>all</td><td>Cases whose id matches, with <code>*</code> and <code>?</code>. Repeatable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--sample</code></td><td>all</td><td>A seeded random sample: a count (10) or a share (0.2).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--first-n</code></td><td>all</td><td>The first N cases in dataset order.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--split</code></td><td>all</td><td><code>train</code>, <code>dev</code> or <code>test</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr id="seq-attempts"><td><code>--seq</code></td><td>off</td><td>Tries a failed case again with feedback, up to K attempts. The pass rate stays the first attempt's.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--feedback</code></td><td>reasons</td><td>With <code>--seq</code>: <code>bit</code>, the failed graders' <code>reasons</code>, or a <code>critic=</code> grader. The expected answer is never shown.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="metrics">Compare two commands that print numbers</h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp metrics</code> runs two commands that each print a JSON object of numbers, such as <code>&#123;"lcp_ms": 2310&#125;</code>.
    The arms run in interleaved pairs. For each metric it prints both medians, the paired difference with a 95% interval, and a verdict.
  </p>
  <Code code={metricsCmd} />
  <div class="doc-table">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>--pairs</code></td><td>11</td><td>Interleaved pairs; each runs A and B once.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--higher</code></td><td>none</td><td>Metrics where higher is better. Lower is better otherwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--fail-on-worse</code></td><td>off</td><td>Exits 1 when any metric is worse in B, for CI.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="exp-replay">Replay one model call</h2>
  <p>
    <code>exp replay</code> sends a call that <a href="/docs/how-to/capture-with-proxy">the proxy</a> logged again, with the model, the system
    prompt or any request field changed. One <code>--vary</code> value changes B and keeps A as recorded; two values set both. A reply passes
    when its first action matches the agent's.
  </p>
  <Code code={replayCmd} />
  <p>
    It uses your own <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> or <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>. <code>--dry-run</code> prints the calls and a cost
    estimate with no key. To replay a whole run up to the call you changed, see <a href="/docs/how-to/replay-llm-calls">Replay model calls</a>.
  </p>

  <details class="more">
    <summary>How the verdict is computed</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>B − A is the change in pass rate over cases both runs graded, with a 95% paired bootstrap interval.</li>
      <li>McNemar's exact test and a sign-flip test use only the cases that flipped.</li>
      <li>Below 100 paired cases, compare also prints P(B&gt;A) from a Bayesian model of the paired outcomes.</li>
      <li><code>power</code> is the smallest change this many cases could detect 80% of the time.</li>
      <li>Runs with a grader error are left out and counted in a warning.</li>
      <li>A matrix adjusts its p-values with Holm's method. <code>--stop-early</code> uses O'Brien-Fleming bounds.</li>
      <li>A sweep uses sequential halving and reports the winner's drop from train to held-out.</li>
      <li>A plateau is called by a restart e-detector over the rounds.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The formulas and their limits are on <a href="/docs/eval-methods">How evals are computed</a>.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="next">Next steps</h2>
  <Cards
    cards={[
      { href: "/docs/how-to/test-agents-md-change", title: "Test an AGENTS.md change", body: "Replay the turns your agent got wrong with your edit, and get Ship it or Hold." },
      { href: "/docs/how-to/ci-regressions", title: "Catch a regression in CI", body: "Run the same eval on the base branch and the pull request." },
      { href: "/docs/judges", title: "Judges", body: "Grade open-ended output with an LLM judge and check it against your labels." },
      { href: "/docs/cli/exp", title: "exp reference", body: "Every `exp` command and flag." },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
